# Gapless fast path, part 0 of 6: the patch-owned state pair.
# Patch state embedded in dead stub-farm bytes (see rationale). The
# initial value is the boot value: no decoder retained. One of 0020-0025
# (the gapless set, in manifest order); 0026 arms it.
name: gapless-0-state-pair
kind: module
module: 0
region: code
address: 0x030515b0
gate: build refuses unless the 8 stock bytes at the target equal `expect`;
  the finished image must also pass firmware_patch.validate()
rationale: Patch-owned state for the fast-path gate (0022): word 0 =
  retained decoder module id (0 = none), with bit 7 set while the
  resident decoder is being reused (module | 0x80). Word 1 is unused
  since the v2 gate (it held the previous sample rate for the retired
  80 ms delay cut) and stays zero.
  The site is two `bx lr` stub slots inside the module-0 stub farm at
  0x030515ac..0x030515b8. An exhaustive reference scan of all 82 modules
  (BL/B.W targets, literal words in code and data, MOVW/MOVT pairs)
  shows zero references to 0x030515b0..0x030515b7, and no fall-through
  can reach it: the preceding stub at 0x030515ae is itself a `bx lr`,
  and the following slot 0x030515b8 (also left byte-identical) returns
  before the live code at 0x030515ba. Because the words live in module
  0's code segment, they are re-initialised from the image on every
  boot: after any reset the pair reads 0/0, so the gate can never skip
  the first load of a session. The code segment is SRAM written by the
  loader at runtime (the same mechanism that loads every overlay), so
  the gate updates word 0 in place. The content is
  data, not code; nothing branches here.
expect: 7047704770477047
replace: 0000000000000000
